# @velobitsio/* is published to npmjs.org.
#
# ⚠️ The scope is not just a string. npm resolves `@velobitsio` to an npm ORG or
# USER of that exact name, so publishing requires one to exist and the publishing
# account to be a member of it. The scope is NOT a label the package picks; it is
# an account name, and there is no config that decouples the two. `@velobitsio`
# must exist as an org, with the publishing account a member with write access,
# before anything here can be published.
#
# ⚠️ How this fails, because the error does not say it. npm answers "you may not
# write to this scope" with **404 on the PUT**, not 403 , it will not confirm which
# names exist in a scope you cannot see. `changeset publish` prints that verbatim,
# so a permission problem reads as:
#
#     E404 Not Found - PUT https://registry.npmjs.org/@velobitsio%2ficons
#
# for a package it is itself trying to create. Two release runs died on that in
# Aug 2026 before it was read as auth. `release.yml` now resolves the token to an
# account and checks scope membership BEFORE the build, so this names itself.
#
# ⚠️ A granular token must grant read+write on the WHOLE scope. Per-package
# selection cannot cover a package the registry does not have yet , which is every
# package in a first publish , and failing that way produces the same masked 404.
# A classic Automation token also works and bypasses 2FA.
#
# `@velobits-dev/*` was the previous scope and the only one ever published from.
# Those packages were removed from npm; nothing forwards, so consumers migrate by
# changing the specifier. `@velobitsio` is the scope from here on.
#
# The shadcn registry namespace is a SEPARATE namespace that stays `@velobits` ,
# it is resolved from a registry URL in components.json, never from npm, so
# `npx shadcn@latest add @velobits/button` is unaffected by any of the above.
#
# Only the registry mapping lives here. The auth line does NOT, deliberately:
# npm hard-fails on an unresolvable `${VAR}` in .npmrc, so committing
# `//registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=${NODE_AUTH_TOKEN}` breaks a plain
# `npm install` for anyone without the variable exported , including this repo,
# which needs no token of its own (every @velobitsio/* dep here is a workspace
# link, resolved locally and never fetched).
#
# Where the token comes from instead:
#   CI       actions/setup-node with `registry-url` writes the auth line into a
#            temporary .npmrc, from the NPM_TOKEN secret. npmjs does NOT accept
#            GITHUB_TOKEN, so that secret is genuinely required to publish.
#   local    `npm config set //registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken <token>` in ~/.npmrc
#   consumer nothing. These publish `access: public`, so reading needs no auth
#            in any Docker build or consumer CI. If they ever go private again,
#            use a BuildKit secret (`RUN --mount=type=secret,id=npmrc`), NEVER
#            an ARG , an ARG is recorded in the image history.
@velobitsio:registry=https://registry.npmjs.org

# Off because provenance needs `permissions: id-token: write` on the release job
# and a supported CI. npmjs DOES support attestation, unlike GitHub Packages, so
# this is now a deliberate "not yet" rather than a hard limitation , turn it on
# together with the id-token permission, never separately.
provenance=false

# Do NOT add legacy-peer-deps here. The editor app's dependency-hardening ADR records why: the root
# `overrides` that pin React/TypeScript are silently disabled by it, and this
# repo is what those consumers resolve their peers against.
